Caching is a technique used to store frequently used data in fast storage (cache)
so the system can retrieve it quickly instead of fetching again from the database,
disk, or a remote service.

In backend systems (Java / Spring Boot microservices), caching:
  - Improves performance
  - Reduces database load
  - Decreases response time

Example
Instead of querying DB every time:

  SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1;

Store the result in cache.
Next request → serve from cache (if still valid).


Types of Caching

1) Local In-Memory Cache (same JVM / app process)

Data lives inside the application’s RAM.

Examples:
  - Caffeine (very common with Spring Boot)
  - Ehcache

Flow:
  Client → Application → Local Cache → Database (only on miss)

Advantages:
  - Extremely fast (no network hop)
  - Reduces DB calls

Disadvantages:
  - Lost on app restart
  - Not shared across instances → can become inconsistent when you scale out

Note: Redis is ALSO "in memory", but it usually runs as a SEPARATE server.
So classify Redis under Distributed Cache, not local app memory.


2) Distributed Cache (shared across instances)

Cache lives in a separate process/server. All app instances talk to it.

Examples:
  - Redis
  - Memcached

Why needed?
When you scale:

  User Requests → Load Balancer → App1 / App2 / App3

If each app has only local cache:
  App1 cache → user name = "Ali"
  App2 cache → user name = "Ahmed"   ← inconsistent

Shared Redis solves that: one cache view for all instances.

Architecture:

                LoadBalancer
                     │
         ┌───────────┼───────────┐
       App1        App2        App3
         │           │           │
         └───────────┼───────────┘
                     │
                Redis Cache
                     │
                  Database

Advantages:
  - Works well for microservices / multiple replicas
  - Shared, consistent cache across instances

Trade-off:
  - Slightly slower than local cache (network hop)
  - Extra infra to run/monitor


3) Database Cache (inside the DB engine)

DB keeps hot data in its own memory.

Examples:
  - InnoDB Buffer Pool (MySQL) ← important, still used
  - Query Cache ← removed in MySQL 8.0 (do not say this is current)

Interview tip: for MySQL 8, talk about buffer pool, not query cache.


4) Client-Side Cache

Cached on browser / mobile app.

Examples:
  - Browser HTTP cache
  - Mobile local storage / in-app cache

Header example:
  Cache-Control: max-age=3600
  → browser can reuse response for 1 hour


5) CDN Cache

Static content cached on edge servers worldwide.

Providers: Cloudflare, Akamai, CloudFront

Used for: images, CSS, JS, videos

Client → nearest CDN edge → origin only if miss


(Optional advanced) L1 + L2 Cache

Many production systems combine both:
  - L1 = local Caffeine (super fast)
  - L2 = Redis (shared across instances)

Read path: check L1 → then L2 → then DB


Cache Write / Read Strategies (Very Important)

1) Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading) — most common

Flow:
  1. Request comes
  2. Check cache
  3. Miss → query DB
  4. Put result into cache
  5. Return data

Spring Boot example:

  @Cacheable("users")
  public User getUser(Long id) {
      return userRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
  }

App code controls cache population.
Also use:
  @CachePut   → update cache after write
  @CacheEvict → remove stale entry on update/delete


2) Write-Through

On write:
  App writes to Cache AND Database (together / synchronously via cache layer)

Advantage:
  - Cache stays fresher; better consistency than write-back

Note:
  - Not "perfect forever consistency" — still need correct keys/TTL/eviction
  - Writes are a bit slower (two stores updated)


3) Write-Back (Write-Behind)

On write:
  App → Cache first
  DB updated later asynchronously

Advantage:
  - Very fast writes

Risk:
  - Data loss if cache crashes before flush to DB


4) Refresh-Ahead

Cache proactively refreshes popular keys before TTL expires.

Good for:
  - Hot / frequently accessed data
  - Avoiding sudden miss spikes


Cache Concepts (Interview Important)

Cache Hit
  - Data found in cache → return immediately

Cache Miss
  - Not in cache → load from DB → usually store in cache → return

TTL (Time To Live)
  - How long an entry stays valid
  - Example: user profile TTL = 10 minutes

Eviction Policy (when cache is full)
  - LRU  = Least Recently Used (common)
  - LFU  = Least Frequently Used
  - FIFO = First In First Out

Cache Invalidation (hardest part)
  - When DB data changes, cached copy can be stale
  - Fix with: @CacheEvict / @CachePut, short TTL, or pub-sub invalidate events
  - Famous line: "There are only two hard things in CS: cache invalidation and naming things"

Thundering Herd / Cache Stampede
  - Many requests miss the same key at once → all hit DB together
  - Mitigations: locking on miss, refresh-ahead, soft TTL, request coalescing


Real Example (Spring Boot Microservice)

GET /users/1

Flow:
  API Request
      ↓
  Check Redis (or Caffeine)
      ↓
  Hit  → return user
  Miss → Query MySQL → store in cache → return

Stack:
  - Spring Cache abstraction (@Cacheable / @CacheEvict / @CachePut)
  - Redis (distributed) and/or Caffeine (local)
  - MySQL as source of truth


Short Interview Answer

Caching stores frequently accessed data in a fast layer so requests avoid hitting
the database every time. Types include local in-memory (Caffeine), distributed
(Redis), database buffer cache, client-side, and CDN. Common strategies are
cache-aside, write-through, write-back, and refresh-ahead. In Spring Boot we often
use @Cacheable with Redis, plus @CacheEvict/@CachePut for invalidation, TTL, and
an eviction policy like LRU. Redis is preferred when multiple app instances must
share the same cache.
